IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `faa-dates`

-- Fatal Accidents Act 1959 (Western Australia) -- the claim the family of a person killed by a wrongful act, neglect or
-- default can bring against the person who would have been liable had the person lived: who counts as a relative
-- (s 3, Schedule 2), when the death gives an action (s 4), who brings it and when a relative may bring it instead
-- (ss 6(1A), (1B), 9), one action only (s 7), the particulars the plaintiff must give (s 8), medical and funeral expenses
-- (s 5(1)), the benefits left out of the damages (s 5(2), (3)), how the damages are divided (s 6(2), (4)), money paid into
-- court (s 6(5)-(7)), being added to an action or separately represented (s 9A), and the Crown (s 11).
--
-- SEAT: the relative of the person who died -- the family member for whose benefit the action is brought (spouse, de facto
-- partner, child, parent, brother or sister, and the others Schedule 2 lists). "My husband was killed at work. Can I claim?
-- Who brings the claim? Can I bring it myself? Will his life insurance be taken off my damages?" A relative who is also the
-- executor or administrator asks the same questions. The court's assessments ("as it thinks proportioned", "such shares as
-- the court finds", "is satisfied") are asked as inputs, never decided.
--
-- Edition: the compilation on the Western Australian Legislation website (mrdoc_49105), retrieved 7 September 2026, last
-- amended by the Statutes (Repeals and Minor Amendments) Act 2011 s 5 (in operation 26 October 2011), as deposited at
-- ../../registers/source-bundle/mrdoc_49105.txt. The Assisted Reproductive Technology and Surrogacy Act 2025 Pt 17 Div 8
-- amends this Act but was uncommenced at that compilation, and is not encoded. No regulation sets a number or date this Act
-- uses.
--
-- NOT ENCODED: s 1 (short title); s 3(1) "court" (a definition no answer turns on); s 9A(3), (4) (the court's procedural
-- orders, and saving its other powers); the compilation notes, except the note that the former s 7 continues for causes of
-- action that accrued before 15 November 2005 (its text is not deposited, so such a case answers "not covered"). The time
-- limit for bringing the action is in the Limitation Act 2005, not this Act, and is not encoded.
--
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Written from the relative's seat with the 2026-10-18 defaults. No pipeline, no coverage table, no
-- independent test pass, no human gate. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Fatal Accidents Act 1959 -- the relative of the person who died`

§§ `Questions this encoding does not cover`

-- The stable l4 has no REFUSE. A question that can reach a gap answers with an `Answer`: either `answered` with the value,
-- or `not covered` with the reason.
DECLARE `Why not covered` IS ONE OF
    `this Act does not say whether an adopted person stays related to their birth family (s 3(2)(a) speaks only of the adopters; see the Adoption Act 1994)`
    `section 3(4) treats only a child of the deceased born after the death as born before it; this Act does not say whether a grandchild or other relative born after the death counts`
    `section 5(1) speaks only of medical expenses that result from the fatal injury and the funeral expenses; other losses are for the court to assess under section 6(2)`
    `section 5(1) speaks only of a death after the Act came into operation on 8 October 1959`
    `section 5(2) does not list this benefit, so this Act does not say whether it is taken into account; that is for the general law of damages`
    `the cause of action accrued before 15 November 2005, so the former section 7 applies to it (Limitation Legislation Amendment and Repeal Act 2005 s 13(2)), and its text is not deposited`
    `section 9A(2) speaks only of a person for whose benefit the action has been brought`

GIVEN a IS A TYPE
GIVETH A TYPE
DECLARE `Answer` a IS ONE OF
    `answered`    HAS `the answer` IS AN a
    `not covered` HAS `because`    IS A `Why not covered`

§§ `Schedule 2 and s 3 -- am I a relative of the person who died`

-- Schedule 2 cl 1: "relative, in relation to a deceased person, means -- (a) a person who immediately before the deceased's
-- death was -- (i) the spouse of the deceased; or (ii) a de facto partner of the deceased who was living in a de facto
-- relationship with the deceased and had been living on that basis with the deceased for at least 2 years immediately before
-- the deceased died; (b) any person who was the parent, grandparent or step parent of the deceased; (c) any person who was a
-- son, daughter, grandson, granddaughter, stepson or stepdaughter of the deceased; (d) any person to whom the deceased person
-- stood in loco parentis immediately before the death of the deceased; (e) any person who stood in loco parentis to the
-- deceased person immediately before his death; (f) any person who was a brother, sister, halfbrother or halfsister of the
-- deceased person; and (g) any person who was a former spouse or former de facto partner of the deceased person whom the
-- deceased was legally obliged, immediately before his or her death, to make provision for with respect to financial
-- matters."
-- "In loco parentis": in the place of a parent (bringing up a child as one's own without being the parent).
DECLARE `My relationship to the deceased` IS ONE OF
    `spouse (married to the deceased when they died, even if separated)`
    `de facto partner`
    `former spouse or former de facto partner`
    `parent`
    `grandparent`
    `step parent`
    `son or daughter`
    `grandson or granddaughter`
    `stepson or stepdaughter`
    `brother or sister`
    `half-brother or half-sister`
    `a person the deceased was bringing up as their own child (the deceased stood in loco parentis to me)`
    `a person who was bringing up the deceased as their own child (I stood in loco parentis to the deceased)`
    `some other relationship (for example aunt, uncle, niece, nephew, cousin, fiance or friend)`

-- s 3(2): "In deducing any relationship for the purposes of this Act -- (a) an adopted person shall be treated as the
-- legitimate child of his adopters; and (b) an illegitimate person shall be treated as the legitimate child of his parents."
-- s 3(3): "adopted person means a person who is legally adopted whether in the State or elsewhere ...".
-- s 6(3): "the relationship between a parent and his or her illegitimate child, and any other relationship traced through
-- that relationship, shall be recognized only if -- (a) in the case of a claim by, or through relationship with, the
-- illegitimate child, parentage is admitted by or established against the deceased parent in his or her lifetime; and (b) in
-- the case of a claim by, or through relationship with, the parent of a deceased illegitimate child, parentage is admitted by
-- or established against the parent in the lifetime of the illegitimate child."
-- s 6(3a): "Subsection (3) does not apply to or in respect of a relationship established by the Artificial Conception Act
-- 1985."
DECLARE `How the relationship is traced` IS ONE OF
    `by birth or marriage, with no adoption and no parent and child unmarried to each other along the way`
    `through an adoption, to the adoptive family`
    `to the birth family of a person who was adopted out`
    `through a parent and a child whose parents were not married to each other, my claim being by or through the child`
    `through a parent and a child whose parents were not married to each other, my claim being by or through the parent`
    `through a parentage established by the Artificial Conception Act 1985`

DECLARE `Am I a relative` IS ONE OF
    `yes: I was the deceased's spouse immediately before the death (Sch 2 cl 1(a)(i))`
    `yes: I was the deceased's de facto partner, living with them on that basis for at least 2 years immediately before the death (Sch 2 cl 1(a)(ii))`
    `no: a de facto partner is a relative only if living with the deceased on that basis immediately before the death and for at least 2 years (Sch 2 cl 1(a)(ii))`
    `yes: I was a parent, grandparent or step parent of the deceased (Sch 2 cl 1(b))`
    `yes: I was a son, daughter, grandson, granddaughter, stepson or stepdaughter of the deceased (Sch 2 cl 1(c))`
    `yes: I am a child of the deceased born alive after the death, and treated as born before it (s 3(4), Sch 2 cl 1(c))`
    `yes: I was a brother, sister, half-brother or half-sister of the deceased (Sch 2 cl 1(f))`
    `yes: the deceased stood in loco parentis to me immediately before the death (Sch 2 cl 1(d))`
    `yes: I stood in loco parentis to the deceased immediately before the death (Sch 2 cl 1(e))`
    `no: standing in loco parentis counts only if it held immediately before the death (Sch 2 cl 1(d), (e))`
    `yes: I am a former spouse or former de facto partner whom the deceased was legally obliged, immediately before the death, to provide for financially (Sch 2 cl 1(g))`
    `no: a former spouse or former de facto partner is a relative only if the deceased was legally obliged, immediately before the death, to provide for them financially (Sch 2 cl 1(g))`
    `no: my relationship runs through a parent and a child whose parents were not married, and parentage was not admitted or established in the lifetime section 6(3) requires`
    `no: Schedule 2 does not list my relationship to the deceased`

-- Whether the relationship is one the Act counts by blood or marriage (paragraphs (b), (c), (f)): the ones s 3(2) and s 6(3)
-- reach.
GIVEN r IS A `My relationship to the deceased`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `is a family relationship of Sch 2 cl 1(b), (c) or (f)` r IF
        r EQUALS `parent`
    OR  r EQUALS `grandparent`
    OR  r EQUALS `step parent`
    OR  r EQUALS `son or daughter`
    OR  r EQUALS `grandson or granddaughter`
    OR  r EQUALS `stepson or stepdaughter`
    OR  r EQUALS `brother or sister`
    OR  r EQUALS `half-brother or half-sister`

-- s 6(3), (3a): whether the way the relationship is traced lets it be recognised. NOTHING where the Act is silent
-- (the birth family of an adopted person).
GIVEN traced IS A `How the relationship is traced`, admitted IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`the relationship is recognised` traced admitted MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF traced EQUALS `to the birth family of a person who was adopted out`
            THEN NOTHING
        IF    traced EQUALS `through a parent and a child whose parents were not married to each other, my claim being by or through the child`
           OR traced EQUALS `through a parent and a child whose parents were not married to each other, my claim being by or through the parent`
            THEN JUST admitted
        OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

-- Paragraphs (b), (c), (f), once the relationship is recognised.
GIVEN r IS A `My relationship to the deceased`, `born after the death` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH AN `Answer` `Am I a relative`
`the family paragraph` r `born after the death` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF r EQUALS `son or daughter` AND `born after the death`
            THEN `answered` `yes: I am a child of the deceased born alive after the death, and treated as born before it (s 3(4), Sch 2 cl 1(c))`
        IF `born after the death`
            THEN `not covered` `section 3(4) treats only a child of the deceased born after the death as born before it; this Act does not say whether a grandchild or other relative born after the death counts`
        IF r EQUALS `parent` OR r EQUALS `grandparent` OR r EQUALS `step parent`
            THEN `answered` `yes: I was a parent, grandparent or step parent of the deceased (Sch 2 cl 1(b))`
        IF r EQUALS `son or daughter` OR r EQUALS `grandson or granddaughter` OR r EQUALS `stepson or stepdaughter`
            THEN `answered` `yes: I was a son, daughter, grandson, granddaughter, stepson or stepdaughter of the deceased (Sch 2 cl 1(c))`
        OTHERWISE `answered` `yes: I was a brother, sister, half-brother or half-sister of the deceased (Sch 2 cl 1(f))`

@export Someone in my family died. Am I a "relative" of theirs under the Fatal Accidents Act 1959 (WA), so that a claim for their death can be brought for my benefit?
GIVEN `my relationship` IS A `My relationship to the deceased` @desc How you were related to the person who died. Pick the closest; choose the in loco parentis options if one of you was bringing the other up as their own child without being the parent
      `the date of the death` IS A DATE @desc The date the person died (ISO 8601, e.g. 2026-03-15)
      `the date we began living together as a couple` IS A DATE @desc For a de facto partner only: the date you began living together in a de facto relationship (any date if you were not a de facto partner)
      `we were living together as a couple immediately before the death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a de facto partner only: whether you were still living together in a de facto relationship immediately before the death
      `that care was still in place immediately before the death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For the in loco parentis options only: whether one of you was still bringing the other up as their own child immediately before the death
      `the deceased was legally obliged to provide for me financially` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a former spouse or former de facto partner only: whether, immediately before the death, the deceased was legally obliged (for example by a court order or a binding agreement) to make provision for you with respect to financial matters
      `how the relationship is traced` IS A `How the relationship is traced` @desc For a parent, grandparent, child, grandchild, step relation, brother, sister, half-brother or half-sister: whether the relationship runs through an adoption, through a parent and child whose parents were not married to each other, or through the Artificial Conception Act 1985
      `parentage was admitted or established in time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Only where the relationship runs through a parent and child whose parents were not married: whether the parent admitted parentage, or it was established against the parent (for example by a court), during the lifetime section 6(3) requires (the deceased parent's lifetime for a claim by or through the child; the child's lifetime for a claim by or through the parent of a child who has died)
      `I was born after the death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were born after the person died (a child of the deceased born alive after the death counts as born before it)
GIVETH AN `Answer` `Am I a relative`
DECIDE `Am I a relative question` IS
    BRANCH
        IF `my relationship` EQUALS `spouse (married to the deceased when they died, even if separated)`
            THEN `answered` `yes: I was the deceased's spouse immediately before the death (Sch 2 cl 1(a)(i))`
        IF `my relationship` EQUALS `de facto partner`
            THEN `the de facto answer` `we were living together as a couple immediately before the death` `the date we began living together as a couple` `the date of the death`
        IF `my relationship` EQUALS `former spouse or former de facto partner`
            THEN `the former partner answer` `the deceased was legally obliged to provide for me financially`
        IF `my relationship` EQUALS `a person the deceased was bringing up as their own child (the deceased stood in loco parentis to me)`
            THEN `the in loco parentis answer` TRUE `that care was still in place immediately before the death`
        IF `my relationship` EQUALS `a person who was bringing up the deceased as their own child (I stood in loco parentis to the deceased)`
            THEN `the in loco parentis answer` FALSE `that care was still in place immediately before the death`
        IF `is a family relationship of Sch 2 cl 1(b), (c) or (f)` `my relationship`
            THEN `the family answer` `my relationship` `how the relationship is traced` `parentage was admitted or established in time` `I was born after the death`
        OTHERWISE `answered` `no: Schedule 2 does not list my relationship to the deceased`

-- Sch 2 cl 1(a)(ii).
GIVEN `living together` IS A BOOLEAN, `began` IS A DATE, `died` IS A DATE
GIVETH AN `Answer` `Am I a relative`
`the de facto answer` `living together` `began` `died` MEANS
    IF `living together` AND `at least 2 years run from` `began` `to` `died`
    THEN `answered` `yes: I was the deceased's de facto partner, living with them on that basis for at least 2 years immediately before the death (Sch 2 cl 1(a)(ii))`
    ELSE `answered` `no: a de facto partner is a relative only if living with the deceased on that basis immediately before the death and for at least 2 years (Sch 2 cl 1(a)(ii))`

-- Sch 2 cl 1(g).
GIVEN `obliged` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH AN `Answer` `Am I a relative`
`the former partner answer` `obliged` MEANS
    IF `obliged`
    THEN `answered` `yes: I am a former spouse or former de facto partner whom the deceased was legally obliged, immediately before the death, to provide for financially (Sch 2 cl 1(g))`
    ELSE `answered` `no: a former spouse or former de facto partner is a relative only if the deceased was legally obliged, immediately before the death, to provide for them financially (Sch 2 cl 1(g))`

-- Sch 2 cl 1(d) (the deceased in the place of my parent) and (e) (I in the place of the deceased's parent).
GIVEN `the deceased was in the parent's place` IS A BOOLEAN, `still in place` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH AN `Answer` `Am I a relative`
`the in loco parentis answer` `the deceased was in the parent's place` `still in place` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF (NOT `still in place`)
            THEN `answered` `no: standing in loco parentis counts only if it held immediately before the death (Sch 2 cl 1(d), (e))`
        IF `the deceased was in the parent's place`
            THEN `answered` `yes: the deceased stood in loco parentis to me immediately before the death (Sch 2 cl 1(d))`
        OTHERWISE `answered` `yes: I stood in loco parentis to the deceased immediately before the death (Sch 2 cl 1(e))`

-- Sch 2 cl 1(b), (c), (f), read with s 3(2), (4) and s 6(3), (3a).
GIVEN r IS A `My relationship to the deceased`, traced IS A `How the relationship is traced`, admitted IS A BOOLEAN, `born after` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH AN `Answer` `Am I a relative`
`the family answer` r traced admitted `born after` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the relationship is recognised` traced admitted
    WHEN NOTHING THEN `not covered` `this Act does not say whether an adopted person stays related to their birth family (s 3(2)(a) speaks only of the adopters; see the Adoption Act 1994)`
    WHEN JUST recognised THEN
        IF recognised
        THEN `the family paragraph` r `born after`
        ELSE `answered` `no: my relationship runs through a parent and a child whose parents were not married, and parentage was not admitted or established in the lifetime section 6(3) requires`

§§ `s 4 -- does the death give a claim`

-- s 4(1): "Where the death of a person is caused by a wrongful act, neglect or default, and the act, neglect or default is
-- such as would (if death had not ensued) have entitled the party injured to maintain an action and recover damages in
-- respect thereof, the person who would have been liable if death had not ensued is liable to an action for damages,
-- notwithstanding the death of the person injured, and although the death was caused under such circumstances as amount in
-- law to a crime."
-- s 4(2): "An injured party who did not commence an action relating to the injury before the limitation period for
-- commencing the action expired is to be taken to be entitled, for the purposes of subsection (1), to maintain an action in
-- respect of the injury if, before the person died, the person -- (a) was not aware of the physical cause of the injury and it
-- was reasonable for the person not to be aware of that cause; (b) was aware of the physical cause of the injury but was not
-- aware that the injury was attributable to the conduct of a person and it was reasonable for the person not to be aware that
-- the injury was so attributable; or (c) was aware of the physical cause of the injury and that the injury was attributable
-- to the conduct of a person but after reasonable enquiry, had been unable to establish that person's identity."
-- s 11: "This Act binds the Crown."
DECLARE `What the deceased knew before dying` IS ONE OF
    `none of the section 4(2) circumstances`
    `did not know the physical cause of the injury, and it was reasonable not to know it`
    `knew the physical cause, but not that the injury was due to someone's conduct, and it was reasonable not to know that`
    `knew the cause and that someone's conduct caused it, but after reasonable enquiry could not find out who`

DECLARE `Does the death give a claim` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the person who would have been liable had the deceased lived is liable to an action for damages for the death, even if the death was caused by a crime, and the Crown is bound (s 4(1), s 11)`
    `yes: although the deceased's own time limit had expired, the deceased is taken to have been entitled to sue (s 4(2)), so the person who would have been liable is liable to an action for damages (s 4(1))`
    `no: section 4(1) applies only where the death was caused by a wrongful act, neglect or default`
    `no: section 4(1) applies only where the deceased, had they lived, could have sued and recovered damages for it`
    `no: the deceased's own time limit had expired before the death, and section 4(2) does not treat the deceased as entitled to sue on these facts`

@export Someone in my family died. Does their death give a claim for damages under the Fatal Accidents Act 1959 (WA) against the person responsible?
GIVEN `the death was caused by a wrongful act, neglect or default` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the death was caused by someone's wrongful act, neglect (negligence) or default, such as a careless driver, an unsafe workplace or a medical error
      `the deceased could have sued for it had they lived` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person who died, had they survived, would have been entitled to sue and recover damages for the injury (leave aside any time limit for this question)
      `the deceased's own time limit had expired before the death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the time limit for the deceased to sue for the injury had already expired, without them suing, before they died (for example an illness from exposure many years earlier)
      `what the deceased knew` IS A `What the deceased knew before dying` @desc Only where the deceased's time limit had expired: which, if any, of the section 4(2) circumstances applied to the deceased before death
GIVETH A `Does the death give a claim`
DECIDE `Does the death give a claim question` IS
    BRANCH
        IF (NOT `the death was caused by a wrongful act, neglect or default`)
            THEN `no: section 4(1) applies only where the death was caused by a wrongful act, neglect or default`
        IF (NOT `the deceased could have sued for it had they lived`)
            THEN `no: section 4(1) applies only where the deceased, had they lived, could have sued and recovered damages for it`
        IF (NOT `the deceased's own time limit had expired before the death`)
            THEN `yes: the person who would have been liable had the deceased lived is liable to an action for damages for the death, even if the death was caused by a crime, and the Crown is bound (s 4(1), s 11)`
        IF `what the deceased knew` EQUALS `none of the section 4(2) circumstances`
            THEN `no: the deceased's own time limit had expired before the death, and section 4(2) does not treat the deceased as entitled to sue on these facts`
        OTHERWISE `yes: although the deceased's own time limit had expired, the deceased is taken to have been entitled to sue (s 4(2)), so the person who would have been liable is liable to an action for damages (s 4(1))`

-- s 6(1A): "Every action brought under this Act shall be for the benefit of relatives of the person whose death has been
-- caused in any manner referred to in section 4."
DECLARE `Can a claim be made for my benefit` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the death gives an action for damages, and an action under this Act is brought for the benefit of the relatives, of whom I am one (s 4, s 6(1A))`
    `no: an action under this Act is for the benefit of the relatives of the deceased only, and I am not one (s 6(1A), Sch 2)`
    `no: the death does not give an action under section 4 on these facts`

@export Someone in my family died through someone else's fault. Can a claim for damages be made for my benefit under the Fatal Accidents Act 1959 (WA)?
GIVEN `my relationship` IS A `My relationship to the deceased` @desc How you were related to the person who died. Pick the closest; choose the in loco parentis options if one of you was bringing the other up as their own child without being the parent
      `the date of the death` IS A DATE @desc The date the person died (ISO 8601, e.g. 2026-03-15)
      `the date we began living together as a couple` IS A DATE @desc For a de facto partner only: the date you began living together in a de facto relationship (any date if you were not a de facto partner)
      `we were living together as a couple immediately before the death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a de facto partner only: whether you were still living together in a de facto relationship immediately before the death
      `that care was still in place immediately before the death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For the in loco parentis options only: whether one of you was still bringing the other up as their own child immediately before the death
      `the deceased was legally obliged to provide for me financially` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a former spouse or former de facto partner only: whether, immediately before the death, the deceased was legally obliged to make provision for you with respect to financial matters
      `how the relationship is traced` IS A `How the relationship is traced` @desc For a blood, step or sibling relationship: whether it runs through an adoption, through a parent and child whose parents were not married to each other, or through the Artificial Conception Act 1985
      `parentage was admitted or established in time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Only where the relationship runs through a parent and child whose parents were not married: whether parentage was admitted by, or established against, the parent in the lifetime section 6(3) requires
      `I was born after the death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were born after the person died
      `the death was caused by a wrongful act, neglect or default` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the death was caused by someone's wrongful act, neglect (negligence) or default
      `the deceased could have sued for it had they lived` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person who died, had they survived, would have been entitled to sue and recover damages for the injury (leave aside any time limit)
      `the deceased's own time limit had expired before the death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the deceased's time limit to sue for the injury had already expired, without them suing, before they died
      `what the deceased knew` IS A `What the deceased knew before dying` @desc Only where the deceased's time limit had expired: which, if any, of the section 4(2) circumstances applied before death
GIVETH AN `Answer` `Can a claim be made for my benefit`
DECIDE `Can a claim be made for my benefit question` IS
    CONSIDER `Am I a relative question` `my relationship` `the date of the death` `the date we began living together as a couple` `we were living together as a couple immediately before the death` `that care was still in place immediately before the death` `the deceased was legally obliged to provide for me financially` `how the relationship is traced` `parentage was admitted or established in time` `I was born after the death`
    WHEN `not covered` why THEN `not covered` why
    WHEN `answered` relation THEN
        IF (NOT `the answer is yes` relation)
        THEN `answered` `no: an action under this Act is for the benefit of the relatives of the deceased only, and I am not one (s 6(1A), Sch 2)`
        ELSE IF `death gives a claim` (`Does the death give a claim question` `the death was caused by a wrongful act, neglect or default` `the deceased could have sued for it had they lived` `the deceased's own time limit had expired before the death` `what the deceased knew`)
             THEN `answered` `yes: the death gives an action for damages, and an action under this Act is brought for the benefit of the relatives, of whom I am one (s 4, s 6(1A))`
             ELSE `answered` `no: the death does not give an action under section 4 on these facts`

GIVEN a IS AN `Am I a relative`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the answer is yes` a IF
        a EQUALS `yes: I was the deceased's spouse immediately before the death (Sch 2 cl 1(a)(i))`
    OR  a EQUALS `yes: I was the deceased's de facto partner, living with them on that basis for at least 2 years immediately before the death (Sch 2 cl 1(a)(ii))`
    OR  a EQUALS `yes: I was a parent, grandparent or step parent of the deceased (Sch 2 cl 1(b))`
    OR  a EQUALS `yes: I was a son, daughter, grandson, granddaughter, stepson or stepdaughter of the deceased (Sch 2 cl 1(c))`
    OR  a EQUALS `yes: I am a child of the deceased born alive after the death, and treated as born before it (s 3(4), Sch 2 cl 1(c))`
    OR  a EQUALS `yes: I was a brother, sister, half-brother or half-sister of the deceased (Sch 2 cl 1(f))`
    OR  a EQUALS `yes: the deceased stood in loco parentis to me immediately before the death (Sch 2 cl 1(d))`
    OR  a EQUALS `yes: I stood in loco parentis to the deceased immediately before the death (Sch 2 cl 1(e))`
    OR  a EQUALS `yes: I am a former spouse or former de facto partner whom the deceased was legally obliged, immediately before the death, to provide for financially (Sch 2 cl 1(g))`

GIVEN d IS A `Does the death give a claim`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `death gives a claim` d IF
        d EQUALS `yes: the person who would have been liable had the deceased lived is liable to an action for damages for the death, even if the death was caused by a crime, and the Crown is bound (s 4(1), s 11)`
    OR  d EQUALS `yes: although the deceased's own time limit had expired, the deceased is taken to have been entitled to sue (s 4(2)), so the person who would have been liable is liable to an action for damages (s 4(1))`

§§ `ss 6(1B), 7, 9 -- who brings the action, and can I bring it myself`

-- s 6(1B): "The action shall be brought by and in the name of the executor or administrator of the deceased person as the case
-- may be."
-- s 9(1): "Where there is no executor or administrator of the deceased person, or where his executor or administrator does
-- not bring an action under this Act within 6 months after the death of the deceased person, any one or more of the persons
-- for whose benefit the action might be brought by the executor or administrator may bring the action."
-- s 9(2): "Any action so brought shall be for the benefit of that or those persons and is subject to the same provisions and
-- procedure, as nearly as may be, as if it were brought by an executor or administrator."
-- s 7: "No more than one action lies under this Act for and in respect of the same subject matter of complaint."
-- Compilation note 1: the former s 7 "continues to apply to causes of action that accrued before commencement day" (15
-- November 2005).

DECLARE `Fatal accidents party` IS ONE OF
    `the executor or administrator`
    `I, a relative for whose benefit the action might be brought`

DECLARE `Fatal accidents action` IS ONE OF
    `bring the action under the Fatal Accidents Act`

-- The six months of s 9(1), as a rule: the executor or administrator has the first 6 months after the death (counted in
-- days from the death); if the action is brought in that time, it is brought for the relatives (s 6(1A), (1B)); if it is
-- not, any one or more of the relatives may bring it. DO, because s 6(1B) says who brings it but s 9 makes the executor's
-- failure a hand-over, not a breach.
GIVEN `days in the 6 months` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Fatal accidents party` `Fatal accidents action`
DECIDE `s 9 -- the executor's 6 months, then mine` `days in the 6 months` IS
    PARTY `the executor or administrator`
    DO    `bring the action under the Fatal Accidents Act`
    WITHIN `days in the 6 months`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST
        PARTY `I, a relative for whose benefit the action might be brought`
        MAY   `bring the action under the Fatal Accidents Act`

GIVEN death IS A DATE
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Fatal accidents party` `Fatal accidents action`
`the s 9 rule for a death on` death MEANS
    `s 9 -- the executor's 6 months, then mine` (`days from` death `to` (`the last day of the 6 months after` death))

DECLARE `Who has brought the action` IS ONE OF
    `no one yet`
    `the executor or administrator`
    `a relative, or some relatives (s 9)`

DECLARE `Can I bring the action myself` IS ONE OF
    `yes: there is no executor or administrator, so any one or more of the relatives may bring the action, for the benefit of those who bring it (s 9(1), (2))`
    `yes: the executor or administrator did not bring the action within the 6 months after the death, so any one or more of the relatives may bring it, for the benefit of those who bring it (s 9(1), (2))` HAS `the 6 months ended on` IS A DATE
    `not yet: the executor or administrator brings the action (s 6(1B)); if it is not brought within 6 months after the death, I may bring it (s 9(1))` HAS `the 6 months end on` IS A DATE
    `no need: the executor or administrator brought the action within the 6 months, and it is for the benefit of the relatives, me included (s 6(1A), (1B)); if I am not named in it, see section 9A`
    `no: one action has already been brought, and no more than one action lies for the same subject matter of complaint (s 7); if I am not named in it, see section 9A`

@export Someone in my family died through someone else's fault. Who brings the claim under the Fatal Accidents Act 1959 (WA), and can I bring it myself?
GIVEN `the date of the death` IS A DATE @desc The date the person died (ISO 8601)
      `the date I am asking about` IS A DATE @desc The date to answer for, usually today (ISO 8601)
      `there is an executor or administrator` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person who died has an executor (named in a will, with probate) or an administrator (appointed by the court) of their estate
      `who has brought the action` IS A `Who has brought the action` @desc Whether a Fatal Accidents Act claim for this death has already been brought in court, and by whom
      `the date the action was brought` IS A DATE @desc If the claim has been brought, the date it was started in court (any date if no one has brought it)
      `the cause of action accrued before 15 November 2005` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the right to claim for this death arose before 15 November 2005 (usually: whether the death was before that date)
GIVETH AN `Answer` `Can I bring the action myself`
DECIDE `Can I bring the action myself question` IS
    BRANCH
        IF    `who has brought the action` EQUALS `the executor or administrator`
          AND `the date the action was brought` `falls on or before` (`the last day of the 6 months after` `the date of the death`)
            THEN `answered` `no need: the executor or administrator brought the action within the 6 months, and it is for the benefit of the relatives, me included (s 6(1A), (1B)); if I am not named in it, see section 9A`
        IF (NOT (`who has brought the action` EQUALS `no one yet`))
            THEN IF `the cause of action accrued before 15 November 2005`
                 THEN `not covered` `the cause of action accrued before 15 November 2005, so the former section 7 applies to it (Limitation Legislation Amendment and Repeal Act 2005 s 13(2)), and its text is not deposited`
                 ELSE `answered` `no: one action has already been brought, and no more than one action lies for the same subject matter of complaint (s 7); if I am not named in it, see section 9A`
        IF (NOT `there is an executor or administrator`)
            THEN `answered` `yes: there is no executor or administrator, so any one or more of the relatives may bring the action, for the benefit of those who bring it (s 9(1), (2))`
        IF `the date I am asking about` `falls after` (`the last day of the 6 months after` `the date of the death`)
            THEN `answered` (`yes: the executor or administrator did not bring the action within the 6 months after the death, so any one or more of the relatives may bring it, for the benefit of those who bring it (s 9(1), (2))` (`the last day of the 6 months after` `the date of the death`))
        OTHERWISE `answered` (`not yet: the executor or administrator brings the action (s 6(1B)); if it is not brought within 6 months after the death, I may bring it (s 9(1))` (`the last day of the 6 months after` `the date of the death`))

§§ `s 8 -- what the plaintiff must tell the defendant`

-- s 8: "In every action under this Act the plaintiff shall deliver to the defendant or his solicitor, full particulars of the
-- person or persons for whom and on whose behalf the action is brought and of the nature of the claim in respect of which
-- damages are sought to be recovered." s 9(2) applies it to a relative who brings the action. The Act sets no time; the
-- rules of court do.
DECLARE `What must I give the defendant` IS ONE OF
    `I must deliver to the defendant or the defendant's solicitor full particulars of every person for whom and on whose behalf the action is brought, and of the nature of the claim for damages (s 8, s 9(2))`
    `nothing under section 8: the duty to deliver particulars is the plaintiff's`

@export I am a relative of someone who died. If I bring the Fatal Accidents Act (WA) claim myself, what must I tell the defendant?
GIVEN `I am the plaintiff` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are the plaintiff in the claim (you brought it yourself under section 9, or you are the executor or administrator who brought it)
GIVETH A `What must I give the defendant`
DECIDE `What must I give the defendant question` IS
    IF `I am the plaintiff`
    THEN `I must deliver to the defendant or the defendant's solicitor full particulars of every person for whom and on whose behalf the action is brought, and of the nature of the claim for damages (s 8, s 9(2))`
    ELSE `nothing under section 8: the duty to deliver particulars is the plaintiff's`

§§ `s 9A -- I was left out of the action (only if an action has been brought without naming me)`

-- s 9A(1): "Where -- (a) an action under this Act has been commenced; and (b) the court is satisfied that a person whose name
-- is not included in the names of the persons for whose benefit the action is stated to have been brought is a person whose
-- name should have been so included, the court may, on application made by or on behalf of that person or of its own motion,
-- order the action to proceed as if the name of that person had been so included."
DECLARE `Can I be added to the action` IS ONE OF
    `possibly: the court may, on my application or of its own motion, order the action to proceed as if my name had been included (s 9A(1)); it has the power, not a duty`
    `no: section 9A(1) lets the court add me only if it is satisfied my name should have been included`
    `no need: my name is already among those for whose benefit the action is stated to be brought`
    `no: section 9A(1) applies only once an action under this Act has been commenced (see whether I can bring it, s 9)`

@export A Fatal Accidents Act (WA) claim has been brought for a family member's death, but my name is not in it. Can I be added?
GIVEN `an action has been commenced` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a claim under the Fatal Accidents Act for this death has been started in court
      `my name is included` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your name is among the persons for whose benefit the claim is stated to be brought
      `the court is satisfied my name should have been included` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court is satisfied that your name should have been included (usually because you are a relative within Schedule 2)
GIVETH A `Can I be added to the action`
DECIDE `Can I be added to the action question` IS
    BRANCH
        IF (NOT `an action has been commenced`)
            THEN `no: section 9A(1) applies only once an action under this Act has been commenced (see whether I can bring it, s 9)`
        IF `my name is included`
            THEN `no need: my name is already among those for whose benefit the action is stated to be brought`
        IF `the court is satisfied my name should have been included`
            THEN `possibly: the court may, on my application or of its own motion, order the action to proceed as if my name had been included (s 9A(1)); it has the power, not a duty`
        OTHERWISE `no: section 9A(1) lets the court add me only if it is satisfied my name should have been included`

-- s 9A(2): "The court may order that any one or more of the persons for whose benefit an action has been brought be
-- separately represented."
DECLARE `Can I have my own lawyer in the action` IS ONE OF
    `possibly: the court may order that I be separately represented (s 9A(2)); it is the court's choice`

@export A Fatal Accidents Act (WA) claim has been brought for my benefit with other family members. Can I be separately represented?
GIVEN `the action has been brought for my benefit` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are one of the persons for whose benefit the claim has been brought
GIVETH AN `Answer` `Can I have my own lawyer in the action`
DECIDE `Can I have my own lawyer question` IS
    IF `the action has been brought for my benefit`
    THEN `answered` `possibly: the court may order that I be separately represented (s 9A(2)); it is the court's choice`
    ELSE `not covered` `section 9A(2) speaks only of a person for whose benefit the action has been brought`

§§ `s 5 -- what the damages include and leave out`

-- s 5(1): "In an action brought under this Act in respect of the death of any person after the coming into operation of this
-- Act, damages may be awarded in respect of any medical expenses incurred as a result of the injury which caused the death and
-- the funeral expenses of the deceased person, if the expenses have been incurred by the parties for whose benefit the action
-- is brought." The Act came into operation on 8 October 1959 (compilation table).
DECLARE `The expense` IS ONE OF
    `medical expenses incurred as a result of the injury that caused the death`
    `the funeral expenses of the deceased`
    `some other expense or loss`

DECLARE `Can these expenses be recovered` IS ONE OF
    `yes: damages may be awarded for them, since they were incurred by a person for whose benefit the action is brought (s 5(1)); the amount is for the court`
    `no: section 5(1) allows them only if they were incurred by the persons for whose benefit the action is brought`

@export Can medical or funeral expenses for my family member who died be recovered in the Fatal Accidents Act (WA) claim?
GIVEN `the date of the death` IS A DATE @desc The date the person died (ISO 8601)
      `the expense` IS A `The expense` @desc What the expense was for
      `it was paid or owed by a person for whose benefit the action is brought` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the expense was incurred (paid or owed) by you or another relative for whose benefit the claim is brought, rather than by someone else (for example an insurer or a friend)
GIVETH AN `Answer` `Can these expenses be recovered`
DECIDE `Can these expenses be recovered question` IS
    BRANCH
        IF (NOT (`the date of the death` `falls after` (`the day` 8 10 1959)))
            THEN `not covered` `section 5(1) speaks only of a death after the Act came into operation on 8 October 1959`
        IF `the expense` EQUALS `some other expense or loss`
            THEN `not covered` `section 5(1) speaks only of medical expenses that result from the fatal injury and the funeral expenses; other losses are for the court to assess under section 6(2)`
        IF `it was paid or owed by a person for whose benefit the action is brought`
            THEN `answered` `yes: damages may be awarded for them, since they were incurred by a person for whose benefit the action is brought (s 5(1)); the amount is for the court`
        OTHERWISE `answered` `no: section 5(1) allows them only if they were incurred by the persons for whose benefit the action is brought`

-- s 5(2): "In assessing damages in an action brought under this Act, there shall not be taken into account -- (a) any sum
-- paid or payable on the death of the deceased under any contract of insurance; (b) any sum paid or payable out of or under
-- any superannuation, provident or like fund or scheme, or by way of benefit from a friendly society, benefit society or trade
-- union; (c) any sum paid or payable by way of pension under the provisions of -- (i) the Repatriation Act 1920-1957, of the
-- Parliament of the Commonwealth; or (ii) the Social Services Consolidation Act 1947-1953, of the Parliament of the
-- Commonwealth; or (iii) the Coal Industry Superannuation Act 1989; or [(iv) deleted] (v) any other Act for the payment of a
-- pension to the spouse or a de facto partner of the deceased, or under any Act whether of the Parliament of the Commonwealth
-- or Parliament amending or replacing any of those Acts; (d) any damages for the pain or suffering of the deceased person or
-- for any bodily or mental harm suffered by the deceased person or for the curtailment of the deceased person's expectation of
-- life that, because of the Law Reform (Miscellaneous Provisions) Act 1941 section 4(2a), are recovered or recoverable for the
-- benefit of the deceased person's estate."
-- s 5(3): "Subsection (2)(d) applies to an action that is, or could be, brought under this Act whether the cause of action
-- accrues before, on or after the day on which the Fatal Accidents Amendment Act 2008 commences (commencement day) but does
-- not apply to -- (a) an action that was finalised before commencement day by a judgment of a court or by agreement of the
-- parties to the action; or (b) a claim, in respect of an action that could have been brought under this Act, that was
-- finalised before commencement day by agreement of the parties to the prospective action." Commencement day: 19 May 2008.
DECLARE `The benefit` IS ONE OF
    `money paid or payable on the death under a contract of insurance (for example life insurance)`
    `money paid or payable out of a superannuation, provident or like fund or scheme`
    `a benefit from a friendly society, benefit society or trade union`
    `a pension under the Repatriation Act 1920-1957 (Cth), or an Act amending or replacing it`
    `a pension under the Social Services Consolidation Act 1947-1953 (Cth), or an Act amending or replacing it`
    `a pension under the Coal Industry Superannuation Act 1989, or an Act amending or replacing it`
    `a pension paid to the deceased's spouse or de facto partner under any other Act`
    `damages for the deceased's own pain and suffering, bodily or mental harm or shortened life, recovered or recoverable for the estate under the Law Reform (Miscellaneous Provisions) Act 1941 s 4(2a)`
    `something else (for example an inheritance from the estate, or a payment section 5(2) does not list)`

DECLARE `Is this benefit left out of my damages` IS ONE OF
    `yes: it is not taken into account in assessing the damages (s 5(2))`
    `no: section 5(2)(d) does not apply, because the action or claim was finalised by judgment or agreement before 19 May 2008 (s 5(3))`

@export My family member died, and the family has received or will receive another payment. Is it left out (not deducted) when damages are assessed under the Fatal Accidents Act 1959 (WA)?
GIVEN `the benefit` IS A `The benefit` @desc What the payment or benefit is
      `the action or claim was finalised before 19 May 2008` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Only for the estate's damages for the deceased's own pain and suffering: whether the Fatal Accidents action, or a claim that could have been one, was finalised by a court judgment or by agreement before 19 May 2008
GIVETH AN `Answer` `Is this benefit left out of my damages`
DECIDE `Is this benefit left out question` IS
    BRANCH
        IF `the benefit` EQUALS `something else (for example an inheritance from the estate, or a payment section 5(2) does not list)`
            THEN `not covered` `section 5(2) does not list this benefit, so this Act does not say whether it is taken into account; that is for the general law of damages`
        IF    `the benefit` EQUALS `damages for the deceased's own pain and suffering, bodily or mental harm or shortened life, recovered or recoverable for the estate under the Law Reform (Miscellaneous Provisions) Act 1941 s 4(2a)`
          AND `the action or claim was finalised before 19 May 2008`
            THEN `answered` `no: section 5(2)(d) does not apply, because the action or claim was finalised by judgment or agreement before 19 May 2008 (s 5(3))`
        OTHERWISE `answered` `yes: it is not taken into account in assessing the damages (s 5(2))`

§§ `s 6(2), (4) -- how the damages are shared`

-- s 6(2): "In every action the court may give such damages as it thinks proportioned to the injury resulting from the death
-- to the parties respectively for whom and for whose benefit the action is brought."
-- s 6(4): "The amount of damages recovered, after deducting the costs not recovered from the defendant, shall be divided
-- amongst the persons for whose benefit the action was brought in such shares as the court finds and directs."
-- Where the unrecovered costs exceed the damages, the text gives nothing to divide; it does not say who bears the rest.

@export The Fatal Accidents Act (WA) claim for my family member's death has recovered damages. How much is there to share among the family?
GIVEN `the damages recovered` IS A NUMBER @desc The total damages recovered in the action, in dollars
      `the costs not recovered from the defendant` IS A NUMBER @desc The legal costs of the action that were not recovered from the defendant, in dollars
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `How much is there to share question` IS
    IF `the damages recovered` GREATER THAN `the costs not recovered from the defendant`
    THEN `the damages recovered` MINUS `the costs not recovered from the defendant`
    ELSE 0

@export The Fatal Accidents Act (WA) claim for my family member's death has recovered damages. How much is my share?
GIVEN `the damages recovered` IS A NUMBER @desc The total damages recovered in the action, in dollars
      `the costs not recovered from the defendant` IS A NUMBER @desc The legal costs of the action that were not recovered from the defendant, in dollars
      `my share as the court directs, in percent` IS A NUMBER @desc The share of the damages the court has found and directed for you, as a percentage (for example 60 for 60%)
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `What is my share question` IS
    (`How much is there to share question` `the damages recovered` `the costs not recovered from the defendant`) TIMES `my share as the court directs, in percent` DIVIDED BY 100

§§ `s 6(5)-(7) -- the defendant pays money into court (only if the defendant has paid money in)`

-- s 6(5): "In any action the defendant may pay money into court as compensation in one sum to all persons entitled under this
-- Act, for his wrongful act, neglect or default, without specifying the shares into which the money is to be divided by the
-- court."
-- s 6(6): "No portion of the money so paid in shall be paid out of court except under the order of a judge, but otherwise the
-- rules of court ... apply."
-- s 6(7): "Where the money paid in is not accepted, and an issue is taken by the plaintiff as to its sufficiency, if the court
-- decides the money is sufficient the defendant is entitled to the verdict upon that issue."
DECLARE `What follows from the payment into court` IS ONE OF
    `the money was paid in as one sum for all of us, and none of it is paid out except under a judge's order; the court divides it (s 6(5), (6))`
    `the defendant is entitled to the verdict on the issue of whether the money paid in was enough (s 6(7))`
    `section 6(7) does not give the defendant the verdict on that issue: the court did not decide the money was enough, so the court assesses the damages (s 6(2), (7))`
    `section 6(7) does not give the defendant the verdict: it applies only where the plaintiff takes issue that the money paid in is not enough`
    `sections 6(5)-(7) do not apply: the defendant has not paid money into court`

@export In the Fatal Accidents Act (WA) claim for my family member's death, the defendant paid money into court. What follows if it is accepted or refused?
GIVEN `the defendant paid money into court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the defendant has paid a sum into court as compensation for all the persons entitled
      `the money was accepted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the plaintiff accepted the money paid in
      `the plaintiff took issue that it was not enough` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If not accepted: whether the plaintiff disputed that the sum was enough and went on with the claim
      `the court decided the money was enough` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If disputed: whether the court decided the sum paid in was sufficient
GIVETH A `What follows from the payment into court`
DECIDE `What follows from the payment into court question` IS
    BRANCH
        IF (NOT `the defendant paid money into court`)
            THEN `sections 6(5)-(7) do not apply: the defendant has not paid money into court`
        IF `the money was accepted`
            THEN `the money was paid in as one sum for all of us, and none of it is paid out except under a judge's order; the court divides it (s 6(5), (6))`
        IF (NOT `the plaintiff took issue that it was not enough`)
            THEN `section 6(7) does not give the defendant the verdict: it applies only where the plaintiff takes issue that the money paid in is not enough`
        IF `the court decided the money was enough`
            THEN `the defendant is entitled to the verdict on the issue of whether the money paid in was enough (s 6(7))`
        OTHERWISE `section 6(7) does not give the defendant the verdict on that issue: the court did not decide the money was enough, so the court assesses the damages (s 6(2), (7))`
